course: dictionary
lesson: 16172
title: Wrongfully
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Wrongfully is an adverb. It tells you how something was done, and it means unfairly or against the law. Adverbs often describe actions, so ask yourself: how was it done?
explain: «The shopkeeper was wrongfully accused of stealing the bike.» The clues are accused and stealing. Being blamed for something you did not do is unfair, so wrongfully must mean unfairly. Test it by swapping in «was unfairly accused»: it still makes sense.
explain: You will often meet wrongfully in news stories and in books about courts and judges. «The man was wrongfully convicted of a crime he did not commit.» Convicted means found guilty by a court, so wrongfully tells you the court was wrong to decide that.
fact: Wrongfully means unfairly or against the law.
fact: Wrongfully is an adverb, so it tells you how something was done.
fact: Wrongfully accused means blamed for something you did not do.
fact: Wrongfully convicted means found guilty by a court when you should not have been.
fact: Wrongfully is a formal word, and you will mostly meet it in law and news.
exercise: «The girl was wrongfully blamed for breaking the window.» What does wrongfully mean here? | Happily, with a big smile | Quickly, before lunch | *Unfairly, against what is right | Loudly
exercise: Which word means about the same as wrongfully? | proudly | quietly | *unfairly | slowly
exercise: What does the phrase «wrongfully convicted» mean? | Lost in a crowd | Sent a letter to the king | Given a prize for winning a game | *Found guilty by a court when it was unfair
test: What does wrongfully mean in «The dog was wrongfully blamed for eating the cake»? | Quickly | *Unfairly, for something it did not do | Loudly | Gently
test: Which word in «She was wrongfully accused of cheating» tells us the blame was unfair? | cheating | she | of | *wrongfully
exam: Tia reads, «The man was wrongfully imprisoned, but his friends proved he was innocent.» What does wrongfully tell us? | He was bored in prison | Guilty, so he deserved it | He was happy to be there | *He was treated unfairly, because he was innocent
